      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To find the length of the longest side of the similar triangle, we first need to determine the perimeter of the original triangle. The perimeter of a triangle is the sum of the lengths of its sides. For the given triangle with sides 6 cm, 7 cm, and 9 cm, the perimeter is calculated as follows:\n\n\\[\n6 + 7 + 9 = 22 \\text{ cm}\n\\]\n\nThe problem states that the perimeter of the similar triangle is 110 cm. Since the triangles are similar, the ratio of the perimeters of the two triangles is equal to the ratio of the corresponding sides. Let \\( x \\) be the length of the longest side of the similar triangle. Then, the ratio of the perimeter of the similar triangle to the perimeter of the original triangle is:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{110}{22} = 5\n\\]\n\nThis means that each side of the similar triangle is 5 times the corresponding side of the original triangle. The longest side of the original triangle is 9 cm. Therefore, the length of the longest side of the similar triangle is:\n\n\\[\n9 \\times 5 = 45 \\text{ cm}\n\\]\n\nThus, the length of the longest side of the similar triangle is \\(\\boxed{45}\\).",
